%I A397817 #36 Aug 18 2026 11:09:11
%S A397817 0,2,4,6,9,11,13,15,18,20,22,24,34,37,39,41,43,46,48,50,52,55,57,59,
%T A397817 62,69,71,74,76,78,80,83,85,87,90,92,94,97,99,104,106,108,111,113,115,
%U A397817 118,120,122,132,137,139,141,146,148,150,152,155,157,167,169
%N A397817 Lexicographically earliest increasing sequence of nonnegative integers such that no two distinct terms differ by a perfect cube.
%C A397817 The sequence is infinite. Suppose a(n) is the last term chosen. Since the gaps between consecutive perfect cubes grow without bound, choose k such that (k+1)^3 - k^3 > a(n) + 1. Consider the candidate m = k^3 + a(n) + 1. Then for every previous term a(i), (k+1)^3 > m = m - a(0) >= m - a(i) >= m - a(n) = k^3 + 1 > k^3. Thus every difference m - a(i) lies strictly between the consecutive cubes k^3 and (k+1)^3, so none is a perfect cube. Therefore m is a valid candidate. Hence a valid candidate always exists, and so a minimum valid candidate exists.
%H A397817 Adam Reichert, <a href="/A397817/b397817.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%e A397817 a(2) != 3 as 3 - 2 = 1^3; a(2) = 4 as neither 4 - 0 nor 4 - 2 is a perfect cube.
%o A397817 (Python)
%o A397817 from itertools import count, islice, takewhile
%o A397817 def a397817_gen(p):
%o A397817     seq = {0}
%o A397817     yield 0
%o A397817     for i in count(1):
%o A397817         powers = takewhile(lambda x: x <= i, (n**p for n in count(1)))
%o A397817         if all(i - t not in seq for t in powers):
%o A397817             seq.add(i)
%o A397817             yield i
%o A397817 a397817_list = list(islice(a397817_gen(3), 60))
%Y A397817 Cf. A030193 (analog for squares), A007412.
%K A397817 nonn
%O A397817 0,2
